4. ACADEMIC ADMINISTRATION DATA MODEL

Inspect existing schemas and reuse compatible entities.

Do not create duplicate tables or competing school identity models.

Every school-owned academic entity must have a reliable relationship to the authoritative school/tenant identity.

Implement or complete the equivalent of the following entities.

4.1 Academic sessions

An academic session represents a school's academic year or equivalent academic period.

Required attributes:

- Internal ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Session name.
- Start date.
- End date.
- Session status.
- Is-current indicator.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.
- Created-by user.

Support configurable session naming, for example:

- 2026/2027.
- 2027/2028.
- 2026 Academic Year.
- Other institution-defined formats.

Requirements:

- Prevent overlapping active sessions where the school's policy prohibits them.
- Prevent multiple current sessions for the same school unless the configured architecture explicitly permits them.
- Validate session dates.
- Preserve historical sessions.
- Do not silently delete academic history.

4.2 Academic terms and periods

Support configurable academic periods.

Do not assume every school uses a three-term Nigerian calendar.

The academic calendar must support:

- Three-term systems.
- Two-semester systems.
- Four-quarter systems.
- Configurable institutional periods.
- Other supported calendar structures.

Each period must contain:

- Period ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Academic session ID.
- Period name.
- Period sequence.
- Start date.
- End date.
- Status.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Requirements:

- Periods must belong to a valid academic session.
- Periods must follow the configured calendar.
- Invalid date ranges must be rejected.
- Periods must not overlap where the school's calendar rules prohibit overlap.
- Historical periods must remain available.

4.3 Classes and grade levels

Implement configurable class or grade structures.

Support different education systems.

Examples include:

- Nursery.
- Kindergarten.
- Primary.
- Elementary.
- Junior Secondary.
- Senior Secondary.
- Middle School.
- High School.
- Grade 1–12.
- Institution-defined levels.

Required attributes:

- Class ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Education level.
- Class name.
- Class code.
- Class sequence.
- Description.
- Active status.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Do not hardcode Nigerian class naming as the only supported structure.

Allow authorized school administrators to define additional classes.

Prevent duplicate class codes within the same school where uniqueness is required.

4.4 Class arms, streams and sections

Support subdivisions of a class.

Examples:

- JSS 1A.
- JSS 1B.
- Grade 5 Red.
- Grade 5 Blue.
- Form 2 Science.
- Other school-defined structures.

Each subdivision must be associated with its parent class.

Required attributes:

- Class arm ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Parent class ID.
- Arm or stream name.
- Arm code.
- Capacity, where configured.
- Active status.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

A school must be able to operate classes without subdivisions if its educational structure does not require them.

4.5 Subjects

Implement subject registration and management.

Required attributes:

- Subject ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Subject name.
- Subject code.
- Subject category.
- Description.
- Education-level applicability.
- Active status.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Requirements:

- Subject codes must be appropriately unique within a school.
- Support compulsory and elective subjects.
- Support school-specific subject definitions.
- Allow subject applicability to multiple classes or education levels.
- Do not assume all schools use the same subject catalogue.

4.6 Teachers

Implement genuine teacher registration and management.

Required attributes:

- Teacher ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Authenticated user identity reference, where applicable.
- Employee or staff number.
- Full name.
- Official email.
- Telephone.
- Employment status.
- Date of joining, where provided.
- Professional qualifications, where configured.
- Assigned classes and subjects through relational assignments.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Requirements:

1. A teacher must be associated with the correct school.
2. Teacher identities must not be fabricated.
3. Teacher registration must use the existing identity architecture.
4. Support invitation and account activation.
5. Do not automatically grant teacher access simply because an email address was entered.
6. Prevent unauthorized school membership.
7. Allow authorized administrators to deactivate teacher access without destroying academic history.
8. Allow teachers to be assigned to multiple classes and subjects where appropriate.

A teacher may have memberships in more than one school only where explicitly authorized.

Do not assume that a teacher's global identity is the same as their school-specific employment record.

4.7 Students

Implement genuine student registration and management.

Required attributes:

- Student ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Student admission number.
- Full name.
- Date of birth, where collected.
- Gender, where institutionally required and lawfully collected.
- Contact information, where applicable.
- Guardian or parent information, where applicable.
- Admission date.
- Student status.
- Current academic enrolment.
- Creation timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Requirements:

1. Admission numbers must be unique within the school.
2. Students must be associated with the correct school.
3. Student records must persist in PostgreSQL.
4. Student registration must not generate fake identities.
5. Support configurable admission-number formats.
6. Support appropriate student status changes.
7. Preserve historical student records.
8. Support transfer, withdrawal and graduation status without destructive deletion.
9. Provide appropriate access restrictions for sensitive student information.

Where students are minors, implement appropriate authorization and data-minimization safeguards for guardian information.

Do not create unnecessary sensitive-data fields.

4.8 Student enrolments

A student record and a student's class enrolment must be separate concepts.

Implement a persistent enrolment entity.

Each enrolment should contain:

- Enrolment ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Student ID.
- Academic session ID.
- Class ID.
- Class arm ID, where applicable.
- Enrolment date.
- Enrolment status.
- Effective start date.
- Effective end date, where applicable.
- Previous class reference, where applicable.
- Created-by user.
- Creation timestamp.

Requirements:

- A student must be enrolled in a valid class.
- The class must belong to the same school.
- The academic session must belong to the same school.
- The class arm must belong to the selected class and school.
- Prevent conflicting active enrolments.
- Preserve historical enrolments.
- Support promotion and progression into a subsequent class.
- Do not overwrite previous academic history.

4.9 Teacher assignments

Implement relational teacher assignment records.

A teacher must be assignable to:

- One or more subjects.
- One or more classes.
- One or more class arms, where applicable.
- One or more academic sessions.

Each assignment must contain:

- Assignment ID.
- School/tenant ID.
- Teacher ID.
- Subject ID.
- Class ID.
- Class arm ID, where applicable.
- Academic session ID.
- Assignment status.
- Assigned-by user.
- Creation timestamp.

Prevent invalid cross-school relationships.

Allow assignment changes without destroying historical assignment records.

